Z||000009||Bazooka||12-25-2001||11:23 AM||||Bam Bam,    It looks like you're selecting someone to powdercoat your engine pieces by the price alone. You could be making a mistake that way. I would look for a guarantee of the work first so that you get a consistent quality surface that's free of flat areas, pitting, fisheyes, etc. It would be a shame to get the best price with an inconsistent surface on your showpiece. Best thing you can do is go with an established, experienced shop and be sure they will guarantee their work. It's not easy or cheap to remove bad powdercoating. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||BAM-BAM||12-25-2001||04:36 PM||||  Thanks BAZ, I feel where your coming from. It's not that I am looking for the cheapest price, the shop I went to is quite reputable. It's just that I am ignorant to what all is involved in Powdercoating and why the price is what it is. I am learning though that Powdercoating can be an art form and the price here has more to do with the fact that the covers and plenum have the machined surfaces that have to be masked off. That is where the cost come in (Labor)<BR>  Thanks all the same and I appreciate all the info that the forum has provided. I am still doing some research.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>85GT 3.4L <BR>Eibachs and Poly's<BR>17" Konig Monsoons<BR>Kumho Ecstas<BR>Interior is all "Mike'd" up||||reg||12
